Jenna and I would work out an outline, sort of like our writing assignment for the day.

507
00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:49.820
So I would know what my goal was.

508
00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:52.160
I would start my goal, start my writing.

509
00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:55.560
And then I would do what I would call the big net.

510
00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:57.540
I would tell Jenna, I'm going to cast a big net.

511
00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:00.040
That meant I had everything in the outline.

512
00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:01.180
I had all the information.

513
00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:06.060
But now I was going to try to think about all the bigger things that would color in

514
00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:06.600
that moment.

515
00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:08.660
I would look through old emails.

516
00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:09.680
I'd look through journals.

517
00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:12.480
I would look at some of the photos and memorabilia.

518
00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:18.160
And I would just try to uncover every little rock, you know, that reminded me of exactly

519
00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:19.680
this moment I was writing about.

520
00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:24.580
And then sometimes as I was remembering a story, I would get up and pace, guys.

521
00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:25.320
I'm a pacer.

522
00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:29.820
I would talk out these moments that I was about to write as if performing stand-up.

523
00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:36.520
For example, Jenna, when we talked about the finale of that very last scene that we filmed

524
00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:41.920
that was going to wrap the whole entire series, when I wrote that paragraph, I just got up

525
00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:44.420
and I talked it out as if I was giving it as a speech.

526
00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:47.220
Then I would record voice memos.

527
00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:48.980
I often would send them to you.

528
00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:51.060
I would say, hey, I just had a brainstorming burSt.

529
00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:54.780
First, I'm going to write this out, but I'm going to talk it out here so we both have

530
00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:56.460
it and we can re-listen to it.

531
00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.120
So those are some of the things that I did.

532
00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:04.200
And I actually really liked, once we figured out our system, because that was a process,

533
00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:09.500
because you were getting emails late at night and messages from me, and I was getting emails

534
00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:10.340
early in the morning.

535
00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:17.780
Once we figured out how to trade that information, I actually really liked it because I would have

536
00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:22.280
all of your thoughts, you would leave me all your emails and messages as I started writing,

537
00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:27.700
and I could process everything that you had done that day, and that would inform me of

538
00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:28.780
my writing assignment.

539
00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:34.440
And then the next morning, you would wake up to emails and information from me, and you

540
00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:37.040
would respond to it as you started your writing process.

541
00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:37.980
Yeah.

542
00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:43.060
But it took us a while to understand how to trade all of that without just feeling like

543
00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:44.720
we were constantly inundated.

544
00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:45.440
Yes.

545
00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:51.240
Well, and through that process, we were also producing our podcast each week, so...

546
00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:54.860
And homeschooling, because this was during the pandemic, guys.

547
00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:55.860
Our kids were home.

548
00:24:56.180 --> 00:25:01.080
I mean, every 30 minutes, I was helping someone, you know, with a math problem or get art supplies

549
00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:01.700
or...

550
00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:04.980
I mean, all I did during the pandemic was give my children snacks.

551
00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:06.060
So many snacks.

552
00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:08.000
So hungry all the time.

553
00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:08.700
I know.

554
00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:14.460
I found that to be, like, a sign of, like, just, you know, unspoken anxiety.

555
00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:16.740
We all just wanted to snack all the time.

556
00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:25.080
The thing you shared that I think I want to highlight, Ange, is how much non-writing writing

557
00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:25.820
involves.

558
00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:34.580
Like, you would spend 90 minutes going through emails and videos and journals.

559
00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:36.100
That is writing.

560
00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:40.160
It wasn't words on the page, but that's part of the writing process.

561
00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:40.880
Right.

562
00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:41.620
The research.

563
00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:42.160
Yeah.

564
00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:44.700
I would have one day where that's all I did.

565
00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:49.460
All I did was, like, gather, gather, gather, and then make notes and gather, gather.

566
00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:52.700
And then it would be the next day that I could then start to form it into a story.

567
00:25:52.700 --> 00:26:02.840
My husband Lee is a writer-director, and most of his day is taking walks, thinking, imagining.

568
00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:07.900
You know, he has to create stories from scratch because he writes screenplays.

569
00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:11.480
And he's writing when he's doing that.

570
00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:16.240
When he's, you know, listening to music for inspiration, that's writing.

571
00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:20.300
So many times we left each other messages because we were like, oh, my gosh.

572
00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:22.500
So it came to me in the shower.

573
00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:23.440
I got it.

574
00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:27.100
Oh, showers are a great place for inspiration.

575
00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:28.160
I don't know why.

576
00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:29.260
And road trips.

577
00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:30.680
I remember being in the car.

578
00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:31.720
Driving.

579
00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:32.260
Driving.

580
00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:35.640
I'm playing out these scenes in my mind of, like, what we want to share.

581
00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:36.500
And I'm like, I got it.

582
00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:42.100
And thank goodness for audio memos on your phone because you can say it.

583
00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:43.980
I would leave myself notes all the time.

584
00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:44.700
So many.

585
00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:49.840
Well, Ange, I'm glad we're talking about this because we got a lot of questions from other writers.

586
00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:51.360
Mariah P.

587
00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:53.300
from Modesto, California said,

588
00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:57.920
I love to write, but I get stuck about halfway through a book every time.

589
00:26:58.140 --> 00:27:02.160
What advice can you give for pushing through those writing blocks?

590
00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:05.880
Because they can be real self-confidence destroyers sometimes.

591
00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:07.540
And Sierra G.

592
00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:09.180
from Kansas City, Kansas said,

593
00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:13.520
Did either of you ever struggle with self-doubt while you were working on your book?

594
00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:17.420
And how did you overcome that and find the courage and confidence

595
00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:21.720
to even try something new, like writing a book or creating a podcast?

596
00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:25.280
She also said, Thank you in advance for sharing your insight

597
00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:28.080
and any practical tips for boosting confidence.

598
00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:31.660
Sierra, I have to tell you, I've written a lot of things by myself,

599
00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:35.680
but writing with Jenna was so healing for me.

600
00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:39.780
Because a lot of times when you write by yourself, you're in this vacuum.

601
00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:43.500
And you can question yourself, like, is this any good?

602
00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:49.600
But Jenna was like this constant soundboard for me, but solid and honest, you know?

603
00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:53.920
And one of the things I love about our friendship is that we can be

604
00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:57.140
constructively, compassionately critical.

605
00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:00.400
I remember so vividly, there was one chapter we were working on.

606
00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:03.900
We worked really hard on it, and then we ended up throwing it out.

607
00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:05.380
We're like, It doesn't work.

608
00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:06.460
It doesn't work.

609
00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:09.040
And you had done so much research for it.

610
00:28:09.100 --> 00:28:12.560
It was like we were throwing away days of your research.

611
00:28:12.940 --> 00:28:15.580
And likewise, though, you had done a lot of work on it, too.

612
00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:18.900
And we both mourned that, but we knew it was the right decision.

613
00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:23.140
I remember we were trading notes, and you were like, It just doesn't work.

614
00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:24.300
And I'm like, No, it doesn't work.

615
00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:24.920
It doesn't work.

616
00:28:25.040 --> 00:28:25.320
Dang it.

617
00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:26.460
And I knew it didn't work.

618
00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:33.220
But I think having someone you trust that can be a soundboard during your creative process

619
00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:34.100
is so important.

620
00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:40.240
I also think knowing that you're going to write a bunch of crap.

621
00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:40.920
Yeah.

622
00:28:41.260 --> 00:28:48.100
Like, I don't ever want to go back and read our first attempt at chapter one, job opening.

623
00:28:48.100 --> 00:28:48.520
No.

624
00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:54.080
Because I've seen glimpses of it, and it is not at all the chapter that it is now.

625
00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:55.540
We had to find it.

626
00:28:55.740 --> 00:29:02.380
And you have to be able to read your stuff and be like, Wow, that's crap.

627
00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:02.900
Yeah.

628
00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:03.980
And throw it out.

629
00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:04.520
And throw it out.

630
00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:05.500
It can't be precious.

631
00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:11.000
And I think also just know that the more you do it, the better you get.

632
00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:12.900
That is really true for you and I.

633
00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:18.600
The more we tinkered on these chapters, because that was the thing I wasn't really prepared

634
00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:19.000
for.

635
00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:23.580
Because when you write a script for television, you might do a few revisions.

636
00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:27.940
But with the book, we did revision after revision, years of revision, it felt like.

637
00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:32.420
And you told me, Jenna, when we started this process, Angela, you're going to read this

638
00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:33.880
chapter so many times.

639
00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:37.080
Like, you're going to read this book so many times.

640
00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:37.660
And we did.

641
00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:39.580
How many times did we read this book?

642
00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:40.020
Hundreds.

643
00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:42.040
Hundreds and have notes.

644
00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:48.400
And so I just think knowing that where you start and where you finish, there's going to

645
00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:49.960
be such a huge learning curve.

646
00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:53.980
Our next question is from Jeffrey in Denham Springs, Louisiana.

647
00:29:54.320 --> 00:29:55.140
What's up, Louisiana?

648
00:29:55.860 --> 00:30:00.540
Jeffrey says, when we read the book, will it be obvious whether Jenna or Angela wrote

649
00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:00.840
it?

650
00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:02.100
Yes.

651
00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:02.820
Yes.

652
00:30:03.020 --> 00:30:05.300
Very obvious because we rotate paragraphs.

653
00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:08.300
We really struggled with how do we tell these stories.

654
00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:12.300
And we decided to tell them the way we share with you here in this sort of back and forth

655
00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:12.780
dialogue.

656
00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:13.660
Yeah.

657
00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:15.340
The book is a conversation.

658
00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:20.180
So you'll see one paragraph will say Jenna and another will say Angela.

659
00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:26.100
And there are times when our memories of a certain event do not line up and we both get to tell

660
00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:28.560
how we remember it, which is really fun.

661
00:30:28.700 --> 00:30:29.440
Really fun.

662
00:30:29.780 --> 00:30:32.860
When it's time for Jenna's paragraphs, her name is above it in blue.

663
00:30:33.020 --> 00:30:33.500
It says Jenna.

664
00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:37.200
My paragraphs above it, my name says Angela in orange.

665
00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:40.680
And my husband, Josh, wanted me to point out that that's Denver Bronco colors.

666
00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:42.120
And was that intentional?

667
00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:43.180
And I said, no, babe.

668
00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:46.640
We didn't pick those colors because of the Denver Broncos.

669
00:30:46.780 --> 00:30:51.100
We actually looked at a bunch of different color combinations and those just both popped

670
00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:51.520
together.

671
00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:53.000
We liked that combo.

672
00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:55.020
Well, Melissa S.

673
00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:57.680
from Trenton, Ontario, and Heather A.

674
00:30:57.840 --> 00:30:59.820
from Virginia both wrote in to ask,

675
00:31:00.080 --> 00:31:02.300
how will the book differ from the podcast?

676
00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:06.380
Will the book have different stories and things we haven't heard before?

677
00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.380
Yes, it definitely will.

678
00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:16.220
You know, this book is our chance to dig a little deeper maybe and especially to dig deeper

679
00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:18.040
into our friendship story.

680
00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:20.500
Yes, it's way more intimate and personal.

681
00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:25.780
Not only do you have all these great pictures and things that we saved, it's like we're giving

682
00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:30.600
you our scrapbook of our time on the office and our best friendship and the journey of

683
00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:31.260
all of that.

684
00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:36.960
We're able to go into detail into such wonderful moments that I'm so glad that my kids will

685
00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:37.620
have this book.

686
00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:41.100
I talk about the time my dad was my date to the Emmys.

687
00:31:41.100 --> 00:31:43.620
It's such a special part of the book for me.

688
00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.260
And there's a lot of moments like that.

689
00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:49.180
It is very different and I'm so proud of it.

690
00:31:49.600 --> 00:31:50.180
All right.

691
00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:53.300
Well, our next question comes from Natalie in New Jersey.

692
00:31:53.900 --> 00:31:57.160
Natalie says, which chapter was the most fun to work on?

693
00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:00.840
Jenna and I both answered at the same time to this one, Natalie.

694
00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:03.900
Award shows and Hollywood parties.

695
00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:11.800
We have a whole chapter about navigating the life of Hollywood and going to these award shows

696
00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:12.860
as best friends.

697
00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:14.820
All of our hits and misses.

698
00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:16.360
There were a lot of misses.

699
00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:20.820
When we were brainstorming writing this, we would sometimes have a writing session.

700
00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:22.120
That's something we should share.

701
00:32:22.280 --> 00:32:26.420
We would get over FaceTime or on a Zoom where we could see each other and we would talk out

702
00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:28.100
some chapters, talk out memories.

703
00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:32.560
There were things we definitely wanted to make sure we shared and we would just talk out all

704
00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:34.700
of our memories and form that chapter.

705
00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:37.160
Award shows was one of the ones we did that.

706
00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:41.740
Our next question is from Sally Jane from Fayetteville, Arkansas.

707
00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:48.320
What were your favorite and least favorite part of writing the book?

708
00:32:48.620 --> 00:32:49.580
I know mine.

709
00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:50.220
What are they?

710
00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:57.800
My favorite part was just reliving some of these wonderful moments in a way that I might not have.

711
00:32:57.960 --> 00:32:58.520
You know what I mean?

712
00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:00.900
Finding all of these pictures, sharing with you.

713
00:33:01.720 --> 00:33:06.120
I laughed so hard I cried at a few of the things that we wrote together, Jenna.

714
00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:09.160
And I don't think I would have written any of that down.

715
00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:15.020
I wouldn't have any of this to share with anyone in my family had we not sat down and

716
00:33:15.020 --> 00:33:15.780
done this together.

717
00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:22.220
And I'm so – that's one of my favorite things is that we have this tangible, amazing

718
00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:25.820
scrapbook memory of our time on the show and our friendship.

719
00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:28.480
And my least favorite is easy.

720
00:33:29.060 --> 00:33:29.460
What?

721
00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:30.440
All the revisions.

722
00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:32.200
All the flippin' revisions.

723
00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:32.880
Revisions.

724
00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:33.720
Yes.

725
00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:35.360
I like revisions.

726
00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:36.560
I know you do.

727
00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:37.500
Thank God.

728
00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:41.480
But like whenever we would get this email, we would get an email that would say, hey,

729
00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:43.180
guys, here's the book back to you.

730
00:33:43.300 --> 00:33:45.100
And it would be like a Wednesday.

731
00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:47.780
And they would say, we would love all your revisions by Friday.

732
00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:48.340
I would think.

733
00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:48.490
Okay.

734
00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:53.080
Immediately I would be like, I have two days of reading from the minute I wake up to the

735
00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:55.560
minute I go to bed, I'm just going to be reading and making notes.

736
00:33:55.780 --> 00:33:56.020
Okay.

737
00:33:56.160 --> 00:34:02.820
I did not like the time crunch pressure of revisions, but I will say my least favorite

738
00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:09.120
part of writing is staring at a blank page and having to start a chapter.

739
00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:10.900
That doesn't bother me as much.

740
00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:15.800
Oh, that to me is the most terrifying part of writing, going from a blank page to something.

741
00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:19.700
Because once I have something, then I can sink my teeth into it.

742
00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:22.080
Then I can take away or add or edit.

743
00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:23.480
I don't mind editing.

744
00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:31.320
But my favorite part of this process, Ang, was the first time they sent us the layout

745
00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:32.220
of the book.

746
00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:32.360
Oh, yeah.

747
00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:33.460
Because that's a whole phase.

748
00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:40.040
After you lock the manuscript, then they send you your book all laid out in a PDF with

749
00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:40.920
all the pictures.

750
00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:45.360
And seeing it, it's written in the font that it's going to be in.

751
00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:45.720
Yeah, yeah.

752
00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:47.440
That was such an exciting day.

753
